WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.709
Second wave feminists I've talked

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.849
to, they're very

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.939
worried about the woke

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.889
gender identity movement because

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.869
it's reducing women to

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:11.219
just body parts.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.569
Like a guy can say, Well, if I just

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.519
get breast implants and I can have a

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.499
vaginal plastic made out of a piece

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.329
of my skin, then I'm

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:19.449
in. I'm the woman, right?

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.269
It's like, well, no, because

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.099
women are not just tits and

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.419
ass, right? I mean, there's more to

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:24.929
it than that.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.819
For decades, Michael Shermer has

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.109
been one of the most popular and

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.509
provocative explicator of science

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.889
to popular audiences.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.509
Having authored bestselling books

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.279
such as Why People Believe Weird

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.139
Things, Why Darwin Matters,

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.089
The Moral Arc and the Mind

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:39.959
of the Market.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.749
He founded Skeptic magazine in 1992,

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.759
hosts a video podcast with leading

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.589
activists and intellectuals and for

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.449
nearly 20 years authored

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.579
a widely read column for Scientific

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.019
American in which he debunked

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.909
beliefs in UFOs and other

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.769
paranormal phenomena, explained the

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.629
rise of the new atheism and

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.179
showed how evolution systematically

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.009
informs human behavior.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:03.449
Shermer's work is deeply and

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.609
explicitly rooted in libertarian

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.559
and enlightenment ideas about

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.329
individual responsibility, free

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.429
market economics, rationality

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:12.869
and the search for something

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.849
approaching objective truth.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.829
In 2019, Scientific

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.719
American cut him loose, a move

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.489
he ascribes to the publication

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.349
suffocating embrace of the

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.999
sort of identity politics and

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.009
wokeness that he says dominates

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.859
academic circles and increasingly

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.269
the culture at large.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.219
Last fall, Shermer, who holds a

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.689
Ph.D. in the history of science and

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:35.759
teaches skepticism 1 to 1

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.529
at Chapman University, started a

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.119
weekly substack where he hosts

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.129
podcasts and the columns he would

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:42.419
have written for Scientific

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.399
American. The 67 year old

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.379
former competitive cyclist talked

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.389
with Reason during Freedom Fest, an

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.189
annual gathering in Las Vegas, about

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.899
the fundamental clash between

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.909
wokeness and scientific inquiry,

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.619
how hard it is to overcome the

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.479
cognitive biases we all

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.309
have. Why he thinks trans

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.289
athletes should be banned from most

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:02.189
women's sports.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:03.779
Why we have so much trouble

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:04.949
acknowledging moral and

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.329
technological progress.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.979
And why he now identifies as a

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.839
classical liberal rather

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.008
than as a libertarian.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:16.509
Michael Shermer, thanks for talking

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:17.199
to Reason.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.029
Nice to see you again.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.039
So you last year.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.749
Last fall, you started a Substack

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.299
publication to go along with Skeptic

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.619
magazine and whatnot.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:29.169
And one of the early pieces was

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.209
titled "Scientific American Goes

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.549
Woke." And you were a longtime

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.499
columnist for Scientific American,

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.929
a very popular

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:39.849
science communicator.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:40.869
What?

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:42.729
Describe what you wrote about

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:44.409
in "Scientific American Goes Woke"

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.719
and why you started a Substack.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.339
Right. So I started Scientific

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.799
American in 2001 and

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.169
I worked almost 18 years, 214

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:54.219
consecutive monthly columns.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.169
I was going for Steve Gould's 300

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.639
that he did in Natural History

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.499
Museum, which would have taken me

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.449
to April 2025, so I didn't quite

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.219
make that. Yeah, they terminated the

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.749
column. You know, there was no

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.129
particular reason given other than,

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:07.989
well, we're going different ways.

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:09.429
And, you know, it's just it's time

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:10.449
for a change.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:11.439
You know, they didn't say, hey,

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:12.819
we've gone woke and you're an old,

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.559
old guy, right?

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.329
So yeah, but I could kind of see the

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:17.439
writing in the wall, last couple of

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.269
columns I had written.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.579
We're dealing with some issues

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.619
kind of related to race and

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.539
and sex and gender and that

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.399
sort of thing. And I could

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.319
feel the uncomfortableness

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:31.179
in the editorial process

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:32.859
and then boom, you know, they they

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:33.909
let it go.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:35.229
So, I mean, I don't know for sure,

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:36.339
but that that was it.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.259
So I, I started the substack

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.239
just because I still had

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:41.649
more writing in me.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:43.089
I like writing the columns,

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:45.159
but this gives me a little more

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:46.509
flexibility if I want to go more

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:48.759
than 700 words like no

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.709
more and. Right, you know, and

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:52.329
also the new media try to keep up

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:54.249
with, you know, I have a podcast and

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:55.959
what everybody's what all the kids

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:57.399
are doing these yeah yeah it's hard

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.019
to hard to know what's the right

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:00.309
path to take. But it seemed like the

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:01.959
substack thing was a way to go.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:03.999
The finances for it,

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.169
you know, are channeled through the

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:06.879
skeptic society, which is kind of my

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:07.989
day job.

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:09.249
You know, so the first one, you

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:10.269
know, Scientific American goes,

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:12.129
well, I recounted my

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:14.289
own experiences, but also

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:16.179
I went through a

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:17.619
bunch of articles they had published

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:19.659
recently that were clearly

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:21.578
what most people would identify

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:23.169
as sort of a Far-Left woke

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:25.389
progressive politics.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.459
And one of the most,

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.559
you know, notorious or

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:31.509
discussed pieces had to do

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:33.579
with E.O. Wilson being

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:34.419
a racist.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.129
Right. Being a scientific racist.

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.809
He's wrote the book Sociobiology.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.109
He you know, in a lot of ways,

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:43.209
you know, his vision of taking

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.459
evolution and seriously

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.199
applying it or trying to apply to

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.209
human society and whatnot had

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:50.799
always been a fraught project.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:51.879
Right. I mean, he was getting

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:53.769
pitchers of water poured over him

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:55.579
in the seventies at conferences.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:57.549
Yeah, but that

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:58.749
could you talk a little bit about

00:04:58.750 --> 00:04:59.750
that.

